MITRE ATT@CK Journey

Lateral Movement (TA0008)


📌 Goal (One Line)

Move from one compromised system to another inside the network.


🧠 Simple Example
An attacker compromises Employee A's computer.
Instead of stopping there, they use stolen credentials or remote administration tools to access:

File Server
Database Server
Domain Controller
Other employee computers

[This is called Lateral Movement]


Common Techniques:

Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)
SMB (Windows file sharing)
Pass-the-Hash
Pass-the-Ticket
PsExec
Windows Admin Shares (C$, ADMIN$)
Windows Remote Management (WinRM)


Important Windows Event IDs:

4624 – Successful logon
4625 – Failed logon
4648 – Logon using explicit credentials
4672 – Special privileges assigned to a new logon
4688 – Process creation (e.g., psexec.exe)
5140 – Network share accessed


What Should a SOC Analyst Investigate?

When you suspect lateral movement, ask:

Who logged in?
Where did they log in from?
When did it happen?
Why are they accessing another system?
Which account was used?
Was it normal behavior?
What processes were launched (Event ID 4688)?
Were admin shares or RDP used?
Did the same account log into multiple hosts in a short time?


📝 One-Line Revision
[ Lateral Movement = Moving from one compromised machine to another within the same network ]


